From 5a3bfee527ac5c91f8c53fc6800e4646fc99a96b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <njohnson@roku.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 14:25:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 07/26] asxxxx, aslink: report identifier truncation instead of
 doing it silently

getid() in aslex.c and getid()/getsymid() in lklex.c all stop
copying at NCPS-1 characters and carry on without comment. The
name the tool retains is then not the name it was given, and
nothing downstream can tell the difference: two identifiers
sharing their first NCPS-1 characters become one. In the linker
that binds a reference to the wrong symbol or merges two areas,
neither of which produces a diagnostic at any later stage - the
link simply succeeds and the program misbehaves. d5e2177 removed
the divergence between the two tools' limits, so truncation is at
least consistent now, but it is still silent, and a name past the
common limit still merges.

Report it at the point it happens, on both sides.

Assembler (aslex.c): getid() and getst() raise a new 'l' error
when input was discarded. getst() is included because it scans
the .module name, which reaches the .rel file's M record and the
debug output. 'l' joins the table in assubr.c as "identifier too
long, name truncated"; the letter was previously unused. Errors
are recorded per line and reported by diag() only on pass 2, so a
truncated name is reported once, appears in the .lst against the
offending line like any other error code, and sets the exit
status through aserr.

Linker (lklex.c): getid() and getsymid() report through a new
static iderr(), naming the truncated result so it can be matched
against the .rel file, and set lkerr. getfid() is deliberately
left alone - a truncated file specification surfaces immediately
as a "cannot open" failure rather than as wrong output, which is
a different and already-visible problem.

Verified:
- Assembler, 260-character identifier: reports
  "?ASxxxx-Error-<l> in line 2 of trunc" with the generic message
  "identifier too long, name truncated", once per offending line,
  on both the .globl and the label definition, in the .lst as
  well as on stderr, exit 2.
- Boundary is exact: 254- and 255-character identifiers are
  accepted silently and written to the .rel in full; 256 raises
  the error. NCPS-1 is 255.
- Linker, hand-built .rel carrying a 300-character symbol name
  and a 300-character area name (the assembler will no longer
  emit either): both the getsymid() path and the getid() path
  report "?ASlink-Error-Identifier longer than 255 characters,
  truncated to ...", exit 2.
- Regression across every target sharing asxxsrc: built both the
  master and the patched assembler for all 56 targets and
  assembled asxtst/tconst.asm with each. All 56 produce
  byte-identical .lst and .rel output, and none raises a spurious
  'l' error.
- Ran the asz80 self-tests tz80.bat and t80.bat end to end
  (asz80 -gloaxf, aslink -xu) with the master and patched tools:
  byte-identical .rel, .lst and .rst.
- Built the linker with -fsanitize=address,undefined and ran the
  truncation reproducer with -m, -j and -y: no sanitizer reports.
- Both tools rebuilt with -Wall: no new warnings.

Not done here: the manual's error-code list (THE ASSEMBLER /
ERRORS in asxdoc/asmlnk.txt) needs an <l> entry, but asmlnk.txt
is generated from asmlnk.rtf and editing the generated copy alone
would put the two out of step. Worth noting that the same list is
already missing <k> and <v>, which the code has had for some
time.

Folded in, asxdoc, asxhtml: document the <k>, <l> and <v> assembler error codes:

The manual's error list (THE ASSEMBLER / ERRORS) describes 16 of
the 19 codes the assembler can actually produce. <k> and <v> have
been in assubr.c's errors[] table for some time without ever
reaching the manual, and <l> is new in d150ace. A programmer
hitting any of the three finds the code on stderr and in the .lst
but no entry explaining it.

Add all three, in the alphabetical position the list already uses:

      <k>   Numerical conversion error:  invalid digit.
            Raised by digit() in asexpr.c when a character is a
            valid hexadecimal digit but not valid in the radix
            currently in effect.

      <l>   Identifier too long:  the name has been truncated.
            Raised by getid() and getst() in aslex.c.

      <v>   Value out of range:  signed or unsigned argument.
            Raised by rngchk() in asexpr.c and by the signed and
            unsigned range checks in asout.c.

After this the manual's list and assubr.c's errors[] table agree
exactly - same 19 codes, none missing on either side.

The manual ships as four renderings of one fixed-width layout,
and asxdoc/ and asxhtml/ carry byte-identical copies of three of
them, so the same change is applied to six files:

    asxdoc/asmlnk.txt    asxhtml/asmlnk.txt
    asxdoc/asmlnk.rtf    asxhtml/asmlnk.rtf
                         asxhtml/asmlnk.htm
                         asxhtml/asxs03.htm

asmlnk.rtf is the same text in a WordPad wrapper - one \par per
line, one \page per form feed, so its logical lines correspond
1:1 with asmlnk.txt and it takes the identical edit. asmlnk.htm
is asmlnk.txt with an HTML header and footer, offset by six
lines. asxs03.htm is a separately generated, de-paginated edition
with &lt;/&gt; escaping and a narrower indent, so it takes the
same three entries reformatted to its own layout.

Pagination is hardcoded in all of these, so the six inserted
lines have to be absorbed. Page 1-67 was exactly full at 49
content lines; the overflow cascades into 1-68 and 1-69, both
also full, and comes to rest in 1-70, which had only 38 of its 49
lines used. The page count is therefore unchanged, and no page
header or table of contents entry needed renumbering. The four
pages now hold 48/49/49/44 content lines, and the break is placed
so <v> and <z> stay together at the head of 1-68 rather than
leaving <v> orphaned at the foot of 1-67.

Verified:
- The reflowed region is line-for-line identical across
  asmlnk.txt, asmlnk.rtf and asmlnk.htm after the edit, checked
  by reconstructing the RTF's logical lines and comparing.
- No page in the document exceeds 55 lines; the page count is
  still 700 and the RTF still has 700 \page markers.
- Page headers after the edited region (1-70 through 1-73) and
  all table of contents entries are untouched.
- The RTF's unescaped brace balance is 0, as it was before; the
  file's raw -65 count is pre-existing and comes entirely from
  escaped \{ and \} in the document's C examples.
- asxdoc/ and asxhtml/ copies remain byte-identical to each
  other.

Not updated: asmlnk.pdf, which is a print of the RTF and cannot
be regenerated here without changing the whole document's
typesetting. It now lags the other four renderings by these three
entries.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
